WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.489 --> 00:00:06.499
May we move then please to item six on the
paper, which is the Corporate Governance Framework,

00:00:06.499 --> 00:00:12.730
which is by no means as bad as it sounds.
Bill, I am afraid all the burden of work is

00:00:12.730 --> 00:00:19.730
falling on your shoulders today. Over to you.
Bill McCarthy: Thanks for your introduction,

00:00:22.529 --> 00:00:29.529
Chairman. We are invited to adopt the refreshed
Corporate Governance Framework. It is an important

00:00:30.419 --> 00:00:37.419
document. It underpins the way we will make
decisions in order to both live up to values

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:42.320
that we profess Ð that will be connected
to patients, improving outcomes Ð and also,

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:48.180
of course, it is part of our formal means
for assuring delivery of the business plan

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:53.960
we have just talked about.
The suite of documents; I am very grateful

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:58.140
to Ed and Margaret particularly for working
with us on the Standing Orders, the SFIs,

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:05.140
and the Scheme of Delegation, which have required
a lot of revision because our functions and

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:12.140
responsibilities have changed in such a significant
way from 1st April. Those documents have been

00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:19.140
incorporated in here, as is in section five
a summary of the proposed new committee structure,

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:25.549
which will support decision-making. I do not
think I will say any more than that, Chairman

00:01:25.549 --> 00:01:31.340
Ð though I am of course happy to take questions
Ð other than commending the arrangements

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:35.149
to the Board.
Malcolm Grant: Ciar‡n?

00:01:35.149 --> 00:01:42.149
Ciar‡n Devane: I have one suggestion around
the terms of reference of the Remuneration

00:01:42.579 --> 00:01:47.979
and Terms of Service Committee; it reads as
if it is largely pay-and-rations for senior

00:01:47.979 --> 00:01:51.810
staff.
Two other things I think we should be looking

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:57.359
at. One is overview of HR policies across
the organisation. It is kind of there under

00:01:57.359 --> 00:02:04.359
3.1.7. The thing that does not come across
at all is succession planning capability development

00:02:04.530 --> 00:02:11.200
for senior leadership, as well. If we were
able to put those two in more explicitly as

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:17.140
objectives Ð pay-and-rations, succession
planning capability, but also overview of

00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:21.300
HR policies Ð I think it would be stronger
for that.

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:25.770
Malcolm Grant: I would be happy to take onboard.
Jo, are you content?

00:02:25.770 --> 00:02:29.040
Jo-Anne Wass: Yes, sure.
Malcolm Grant: Any other comments? Yes, Naguib?

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:34.660
Naguib Kheraj: Could I suggest it is also
explicit about the authorisation process for

00:02:34.660 --> 00:02:40.470
senior appointments, too: which ones would
be signed off by the committee, which ones

00:02:40.470 --> 00:02:47.210
would go to the Board, and which are delegated
to management? I did not find that in the

00:02:47.210 --> 00:02:49.310
document.
Malcolm Grant: No, you are quite right; I

00:02:49.310 --> 00:02:56.310
think that needs to be spelt out crystal clear.
Thank you. Right, I think we approve the Corporate

00:02:56.490 --> 00:03:02.810
Governance Framework. Thank you very much.
We move now to feedback from Board Committees.

00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:08.260
I understand the CSU Committee has not met
since our last meeting, but I would like to

00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:14.880
invite Victor to give us an update on the
CCG Committee, and Bruce to have a few words

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:17.780
about the Quality and Clinical Risk Committee.
Victor?

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:24.010
Victor Adebowale: On the CCG sub-committee,
we have moved from authorisation to development.

00:03:24.010 --> 00:03:30.800
We have either authorised or partly authorised
211 CCGs. Can I thank and congratulate the

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:37.800
staff for supporting us in doing that, because
it was no mean feat. We are looking forward

00:03:39.830 --> 00:03:45.900
to the next phase, which is helping those
CCGs that did not get full authorisation achieve

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:49.640
full authorisation, and developing. I do not
know whether either Ciar‡n or Naguib wants

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:50.720
to add anything?
Ciar‡n Devane: Just to reiterate: great

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:57.720
work, and the people who have participated
in the authorisation as well. The two I sat

00:03:57.770 --> 00:04:01.770
in on were excellent.
Victor Adebowale: Yes.

00:04:01.770 --> 00:04:06.790
Malcolm Grant: We should not underestimate
what was actually achieved in the start-up

00:04:06.790 --> 00:04:13.790
phase of bringing 211 CCGs through in a highly
supportive and development process, from early

00:04:14.540 --> 00:04:20.069
stages into fully operational organisations
with absolutely key responsibility in the

00:04:20.069 --> 00:04:25.180
new structures.
Victor Adebowale: I did make a point that

00:04:25.180 --> 00:04:31.599
I think we have done the best we could possibly
do given the constraints of time, resource

00:04:31.599 --> 00:04:37.229
and legislation. We made that point at the
first meeting. I think we have done a brilliant

00:04:37.229 --> 00:04:40.840
job.
Malcolm Grant: I think we would like to commend

00:04:40.840 --> 00:04:44.240
you, Barbara, but also ask you to pass on
our thanks to the team who have been responsible

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:51.219
for this work. Thank you very much.
That then brings us to Bruce and the proposed

00:04:51.219 --> 00:04:57.340
Quality and Clinical Risk Committee.
Thank you. We are still deliberating on aspects

00:04:57.340 --> 00:05:04.340
of that. This will essentially be overseen
by Jane Cummings as Chief Nursing Officer,

00:05:05.499 --> 00:05:11.689
and myself. The issues that we are tackling
at the moment are, how does such a committee

00:05:11.689 --> 00:05:18.499
deal with clinical risk in a very diverse
system where patients interact with providers,

00:05:18.499 --> 00:05:23.580
and yet we oversee a commissioning system?
That is one of the first issues we are tackling.

00:05:23.580 --> 00:05:28.879
The second is, within that context, how do
we define our terms of reference? We hope

00:05:28.879 --> 00:05:34.330
that we will have those by the middle of next
week. We think that they are likely to be

00:05:34.330 --> 00:05:41.319
based on our statutory duties of improving
quality of both directly and indirectly commissioned

00:05:41.319 --> 00:05:48.319
services, and a focus on the early identification
of risk across the system. That raises issues

00:05:49.090 --> 00:05:53.979
of how this committee interacts with others,
of course, and its membership.

00:05:53.979 --> 00:06:00.979
In terms of membership, we are keen to have
involvement of other clinical organisations

00:06:01.879 --> 00:06:07.479
who will have a view on risk and who may feel
impacted by clinical risk, so that they can

00:06:07.479 --> 00:06:13.240
advise us from an independent point of view.
That then raises the issue of the Chair, as

00:06:13.240 --> 00:06:18.680
to whether or not the Chair of the Committee
should come from within the Board, or whether

00:06:18.680 --> 00:06:25.680
we should be looking for a completely independent
and experienced clinician to chair the Board.

00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:33.340
In any event, we hope that we will have resolved
most of these issues by the middle of next

00:06:33.340 --> 00:06:38.419
week, for colleagues to sign off.
Malcolm Grant: Thank you, Bruce. Right, I

00:06:38.419 --> 00:06:44.270
think that then brings us to the end of the
item on updates from Board Committees. That

00:06:44.270 --> 00:06:48.710
brings us to any other business. The date
of the next meeting is noted on the agenda;

00:06:48.710 --> 00:06:55.710
it will be in May, in Quarry House in Leeds.
I have only one other item of other business,

00:06:55.979 --> 00:07:00.770
which is to note that this will be the last
meeting attended by Ian Dalton. We are very

00:07:00.770 --> 00:07:06.389
sorry to lose you, Ian, and we would all wish
to do two things. One is to thank you for

00:07:06.389 --> 00:07:13.169
all your contribution in bringing what was
the NHS Commissioning Board into fruition

00:07:13.169 --> 00:07:18.309
as NHS England. The second is to extend our
warmest best wishes to you in your future

00:07:18.309 --> 00:07:21.770
career.
Ian Dalton: Thank you very much.

00:07:21.770 --> 00:07:28.770
Malcolm Grant: I will just say in conclusion
that the Board will now have a private meeting,

00:07:29.089 --> 00:07:33.789
and we will move into business that is confidential.
May I thank everybody who has joined us this

00:07:33.789 --> 00:07:37.469
morning for this meeting. I declare the meeting
closed. Thank you.

00:07:37.469 --> 00:07:37.719
NHS England Board Meeting Friday, 12th April
2013

